{"p":"/luau/queues","t":"Queues","d":"Queues are data structures that follow the First In First Out (FIFO) principle.","h":"<p>A queue is a linear data structure with a collection of items. There are two types of queues on Roblox: <a href=\"#regular-queues\">regular queues</a>, which follow the first-in-first-out (FIFO) principle, and <a href=\"#priority-queues\">priority queues</a>, which have priorities for items in the queue that determine the data accessing order. Items in both types of queues can be any Luau <a href=\"#/docs/luau\">data type</a>.</p>\n<p>Queue is a built-in data structure of the <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/cloud-services/memory-stores/queue\">non-persistent data storage</a> service named <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/MemoryStoreService\"><code>MemoryStoreService</code></a>, for which you can directly call the built-in functions to get a queue and add, read, or remove data from the queue. For any other usage, such as scheduling tasks and handling requests in your game, you can use tables to implement queues on your own.</p>\n<h2>Regular queues</h2>\n<p>Regular queues are maintained in the FIFO sequence, in which all items are added to the back of the queue and read or removed in the same order as they are added, from the front to the end.</p>\n<figure>\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" />\n  <figcaption>The order of how a regular queue adds, reads, and removes items</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2>Priority queues</h2>\n<p>Priority queues are not following the FIFO rule, in which each item can be added with a priority number that indicates its order being read or removed. The item at the back of a priority queue has the default priority of 0, and the item at the front of the queue has the highest set priority, which is 5 in the following example.</p>\n<figure>\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" />\n  <figcaption>An item's set priority changes the order in which a queue reads items</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>For this example, an item with a set priority of 3 is being added to a queue. The queue places the new item behind all existing items with the set priority of 3 or more. To place an item at the front of the queue, you need to set the priority higher than the current highest set priority. In this example, you need to set the priority to 6 or higher.</p>\n<p>Priority queues are useful for situations where you want to read or access data based on the order of importance instead of the order of being added. You can set a priority as an integer when adding an item, and the queue processes items with higher priority before items with lower priorities. For example, you can use priority queues for matchmaking by assigning higher priorities to users who have been waiting for a long time.</p>\n<h2>Implement queues</h2>\n<p>You can use built-in queues of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/MemoryStoreService\"><code>MemoryStoreService</code></a> or use <a href=\"#/docs/luau/tables\">tables</a> to implement queues for all other usage. The following code sample shows the implementation a <strong>regular queue</strong>. To use this implementation for your game, you should save it as a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ModuleScript\"><code>ModuleScript</code></a> and store it in <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedStorage\"><code>ReplicatedStorage</code></a>, so your queue is accessible for both client and server.</p>\n<p><strong>Implementing a Regular Queue Using Table</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">--!strict\nlocal Queue = {}\nQueue.__index = Queue\n\nexport type Queue&lt;T&gt; = typeof(setmetatable(\n    {} :: {\n        _first: number,\n        _last: number,\n        _queue: { T },\n    },\n    Queue\n))\n\nfunction Queue.new&lt;T&gt;(): Queue&lt;T&gt;\n    local self = setmetatable({\n        _first = 0,\n        _last = -1,\n        _queue = {},\n    }, Queue)\n\n    return self\nend\n\n-- Check if the queue is empty\nfunction Queue.isEmpty&lt;T&gt;(self: Queue&lt;T&gt;)\n    return self._first &gt; self._last\nend\n\n-- Add a value to the queue\nfunction Queue.enqueue&lt;T&gt;(self: Queue&lt;T&gt;, value: T)\n    local last = self._last + 1\n    self._last = last\n    self._queue[last] = value\nend\n\n-- Remove a value from the queue\nfunction Queue.dequeue&lt;T&gt;(self: Queue&lt;T&gt;): T\n    if self:isEmpty() then\n        error(&quot;Cannot dequeue from empty queue&quot;)\n    end\n\n    local first = self._first\n    local value = self._queue[first]\n    self._queue[first] = nil\n    self._first = first + 1\n\n    return value\nend\n\nreturn Queue\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following code sample is a usage example as a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Script\"><code>Script</code></a> under <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Workspace\"><code>Workspace</code></a>. You can modify the code, type, and storage location to fit your own usage, as long as you have the previous implementation code sample properly stored.</p>\n<p><strong>Regular Queue Usage Example</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(&quot;ReplicatedStorage&quot;)\nlocal Queue = require(ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(&quot;Queue&quot;))\n\nlocal myQueue = Queue.new()\n\n-- Add some values to the queue\nmyQueue:enqueue(5)\nmyQueue:enqueue(10)\nmyQueue:enqueue(15)\n\n-- myQueue = { 5, 10, 15 }\n\n-- Remove one value from the queue\nlocal first = myQueue:dequeue()\nprint(&quot;The first value added to the queue was&quot;, first)\n\n-- myQueue = { 10, 15 }\n\n-- Add more values to the queue\nmyQueue:enqueue(20)\nmyQueue:enqueue(25)\nmyQueue:enqueue(30)\n\n-- myQueue = { 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 }\n\n-- Remove another value from the queue\nlocal second = myQueue:dequeue()\nprint(&quot;The second value added to the queue was&quot;, second)\n\n-- myQueue = { 15, 20, 25, 30 }\n</code></pre>","kid":{"simple":"A queue is a line of items where the first one in is the first one out, just like a line at a snack stand. Games use queues for things like players waiting for a match or tasks waiting their turn. There is also a priority queue, where more important items jump ahead. Luau does not have a queue type built in, but you can make one with a table.","analogy":"A regular queue is the line for a ride: whoever got there first gets on first. A priority queue is like a line where VIP pass holders get to go ahead of everyone else.","keyPoints":["A regular queue follows FIFO: first in, first out.","New items are added to the back, and items are read or removed from the front.","In a priority queue, each item has a priority number. Higher priority items come out first.","In a priority queue, a new item goes behind all items with the same or higher priority. To jump to the very front, give it a higher priority than the current highest.","Priority queues help with matchmaking, for example giving players who waited longest a higher priority.","`MemoryStoreService` has built-in queues for sharing data across servers. For anything else, build a queue with a table.","The page's queue module has `enqueue` (add to back), `dequeue` (take from front), and `isEmpty`."],"terms":{"queue":"A list where items join at the back and leave from the front.","FIFO":"First In, First Out. The item added first is the first one removed.","enqueue":"To add an item to the back of the queue.","dequeue":"To remove the item at the front of the queue.","priority queue":"A queue where items with bigger priority numbers come out first, no matter when they were added.","priority":"A number that says how important an item is. With priorities 0, 3, and 5, the 5 comes out first.","linear data structure":"Items arranged in a single line, one after another.","MemoryStoreService":"A Roblox service for short-term data shared between all your game's servers. It includes built-in queues.","ModuleScript":"A script that holds code other scripts can load with `require`."},"example":{"title":"A simple waiting line for a mini-game","place":"Script in ServerScriptService","code":"-- A simple queue using a table and two counters\nlocal queue = {}\nlocal first = 1 -- position of the front item\nlocal last = 0  -- position of the back item\n\nlocal function enqueue(value)\n\tlast += 1\n\tqueue[last] = value\nend\n\nlocal function isEmpty()\n\treturn first > last\nend\n\nlocal function dequeue()\n\tif isEmpty() then\n\t\treturn nil\n\tend\n\tlocal value = queue[first]\n\tqueue[first] = nil -- clear the old spot\n\tfirst += 1\n\treturn value\nend\n\nenqueue(\"Ava\")\nenqueue(\"Ben\")\nenqueue(\"Cho\")\n\nprint(dequeue()) --> Ava (first in, first out)\nprint(dequeue()) --> Ben\nenqueue(\"Dev\")\nprint(dequeue()) --> Cho\nprint(dequeue()) --> Dev\nprint(dequeue()) --> nil (the line is empty)","explain":["`first` and `last` remember where the front and back of the line are.","`enqueue` moves `last` up by 1 and puts the new name there.","`dequeue` takes the name at `first`, clears that spot, and moves `first` up by 1.","Names come out in the same order they went in: Ava, Ben, Cho, then Dev.","When the line is empty, `dequeue` returns `nil` instead of crashing."]},"watchOut":["Removing from an empty queue. Check `isEmpty()` first, or the page's module will throw an error.","Using `table.remove(list, 1)` on very long lists. It shifts every item, which gets slow; the two-counter method avoids that.","Mixing up queues and stacks. A queue gives you the oldest item; a stack gives you the newest."]}}